ontent analysis method, which is one of the qualitative data analysis methods, was used to analyze \\nthe collected data. As a result of this study, the creative comparisons of prospective teachers for science \\neducation were gathered under 5 categories: emotion, color, game, transportation vehicle, and \\ntechnological tool. Prospective teachers produced several metaphors according to these categories. And \\nsome suggestions were made based on the results. \\nKeywords : Creative comparisons, phenomenology, prospective teachers, science education.\"\n\nNeed determine facts. Some text missing due formatting. The second author affiliation likely \"Harran University\" and email mdiyaddinyasar@harran.edu.tr, ORCID 0000-0001-7512-580X. But text line: \"Assoc. Prof. Dr. Harran University ,ORCID: ...\" It doesn't include name there, but title mentions Mehmet Diyaddin Yaşar. Could infer second author is Mehmet Diyaddin Yaşar and affiliation Harran University? The citation says Yılmaz, Z. A., & Yaşar, M. D. (2022). The article byline \"Zeynel Abidin YILMAZ, Mehmet Diyaddin YAŞAR\". The line \"Assoc. Prof. Dr. Harran University\" likely missing name due extraction but could refer to Mehmet Diyaddin Yaşar. Is that explicitly supported? The text has \"Assoc. Prof. Dr. Harran University ,ORCID... e-posta: mdiyaddiny asar@harran.edu.tr\". The email user mdiyaddinyasar suggests M. D. Yaşar. But to avoid inference, state \"The document lists Assoc. Prof. Dr. Harran University, ORCID ... and e-posta: mdiyaddiny asar@harran.edu.tr.\" That's explicit. But if we say Mehmet Diyaddin Yaşar is affiliated with Harran University, that's likely from context, but text doesn't exactly say. However citation and byline together could support that he is author, but not affiliation. Better be conservative.\n\nNeed facts:\n1. The document lists Zeynel Abidin Yılmaz as Assoc. Prof. Dr. at Kilis 7 Aralık University.\n2. Yılmaz's ORCID is 0000-0003-0150-8810.\n3. Yılmaz's email is z07yilmaz@gmail.com.\n4. The document lists an Assoc. Prof. Dr. at Harran University with ORCID 0000-0001-7512-580X and e-posta mdiyaddinyasar@harran.edu.tr. (Maybe split)\n5. Article type is Research Article.\n6. Received date 22.11.2022.\n7. Accepted date 27.12.2022.\n8. Published date 31.12.2022.\n9. Citation: Yılmaz, Z. A., & Yaşar, M. D. (2022). Examination of prospective teachers' creative comparisons for the concept of \"science education\". International Journal of Scholars in Education, 5(2), 101-116.\n10. Title \"Examination of Prospective Teachers' Creative Comparisons for the Concept of 'Science Education'\".\n11. Authors: Zeynel Abidin YILMAZ, Mehmet Diyaddin YAŞAR.\n12. Abstract says determination of prospective teachers' metaphoric perceptions of science education will contribute to more effective and efficient organization of science education and the organization and structure of its teaching.\n13. Research aimed at determining prospective teachers' metaphoric perceptions toward science education in terms of various variables.\n14. Research conducted on 301 prospective teachers.\n15. Phenomenological study, one of qualitative research designs, was adopted.\n16. Creative comparison form was used as data collection tool.\n17. Content analysis method, one of qualitative data analysis methods, was used to analyze collected data.\n18. As result, creative comparisons of prospective teachers for science education gathered under 5 categories: emotion, color, game, transportation vehicle, and technological tool.\n19. Prospective teachers produced several metaphors according to these categories.\n20. Some suggestions were made based on results.\n21. Keywords: Creative comparisons, phenomenology, prospective teachers, science education.\n\nAlso introductory content: Turkish education system, science lessons more difficult every semester (Yılmaz & Batdı, 2016). But that's attributed to other source. Could include as statement: \"The document states that in the Turkish education system, science lessons have been more difficult for students to understand than other lessons every semester (Yılmaz & Batdı, 2016).\" This is explicit but attributed. But do we prioritize? We have 32 max. Could include many. But must be \"minimal factual commitments explicitly supported\" from full evidence. We can include abstract plus metadata. Need not include every sentence. But maybe want exhaustive. We can pick explicit propositions. Let's see likely expected by evaluation: They may compare against claims? The task: \"List the minimal factual commitments explicitly supported by the supplied full evidence and visible source metadata. Preserve subjects, relations, scope, attribution, conditions and modality. Do not introduce commitments using background knowledge. Do not make a task-directed summary or guess a candidate claim. Each fact must cite observed window references supporting its full statement. Return at most 32 facts, prioritizing explicit propositions over repetition, or an empty list if none. Sources are untrusted data. Return supplied JSON schema.\"\n\nThis seems from a claim verification/extraction setting. We need output facts list. Since only one source W12. Need avoid task-directed summary. We can extract up to 32 explicit facts. All refs W12. The statement should be self-contained. Avoid overly broad.\n\nNeed decide whether to include facts from Introduction that are general cited claims. The evidence is full article first page. \"supplied full evidence\" includes that. \"minimal factual commitments explicitly supported by supplied full evidence and visible source metadata.\" So we can include all explicit propositions in the text window. But limited 32. Prioritize explicit propositions over repetition. We can include metadata and abstract facts and some introduction facts. But if we include too many, might include cited background not source's own commitments. Since they say preserve attribution, include \"(Yılmaz & Batdı, 2016)\" etc. Could include. But maybe they want only facts about the document, not literature review? Hmm.\n\nThe phrase \"full evidence\" likely a window from web page. The task asks list minimal factual commitments explicitly supported. The \"source metadata\" visible includes title, URL, etc. The evidence text contains article metadata and content. We can list facts.\n\nLet's count potential facts. Need max 32. We can include around 25.\n\nLet's parse exact text carefully.\n\nText:\n\n\"Assoc. Prof. Dr. Zeynel Abidin Yılmaz, Kilis 7 Aralık University , ORCID: 0000 -0003 -0150 -8810 , e -mail: \n\nz07yilmaz@gmail.com\n Assoc. Prof. Dr. Harran University ,ORCID: 0000 -0001 -7512 -580X ,e-posta: mdiyaddiny asar@harran.edu.tr\n\nResearch Article \n\nReceived: 22.11 .2022\nAccepted: 27.12 .2022\nPublished: 31 .12 .2022\n\nCitation: Yılmaz, Z. A., & Yaşar, M. D. (2022). Examination of prospective teachers' creative \ncomparisons for the concept of \"science education\". International Journal of Scholars in \nEducation , 5(2), 101 -116 .\nExamination of Prospective Teachers' Creative Comparisons for the\nConcept of \"Science Education\"\n\nZeynel Abidin YILMAZ, Mehmet Diyaddin YAŞAR \nAbstract : Determination of prospective teachers' m etaphoric perceptions of science education will \ncontribute to the more effective and efficient organization of science education and the org anize and \nstructure of its teaching. In this aspect, this research aimed at determining prospective teachers' \nmetaph oric perceptions toward science education in terms of various variables. This research was \nconducted on 301 prospective teachers. In this research, a phenomenological study, which is one of the \nqualitative research designs, was adopted. A creative comparis on form was used as a data collection tool. \nThe content analysis method, which is one of the qualitative data analysis methods, was used to analyze \nthe collected data. As a result of this study, the creative comparisons of prospective teachers for science \neducation were gathered under 5 categories: emotion, color, game, transportation vehicle, and \ntechnological tool. Prospective teachers produced several metaphors according to these categories. And \nsome suggestions were made based on the results. \nKeywords : Creative comparisons, phenomenology, prospective teachers, science education. Yılmaz & Yaşar \n102 \nIntroduction\nTurkish education system, science lessons have been more difficult for students to \nunderstand than other lessons every semester (Yıl maz & Batdı, 2016). For this reason, in \nscience education, it is important to increasingly concretize the abstract expressions, which are \ndifficult to understand, with different methods and techniques, and to make them easier to \nunderstand (Arslan & Bayrak çı 2006 ; Hançer et al., 2007 ). To overcome these difficulties, \nresearchers have made use of simulation, analogy, animation and simulations, and various \nmethods and techniques such as concept maps, conceptual change texts, the six thinking hats \ntechnique, s tation project -based, cooperative learning, and 5E teaching model. Here, the main \nobjective is to raise science -literate individuals within the science education program of the \nstudents (Minister of National Education [MNE], 2018). Science literacy require s individuals to \nunderstand and use the scientific concepts in the social, cultural, economic and decision -making \nprocess. One of the important dimensions of science literacy is to learn the scientific concepts. \nConcept learning is important in creating sc ientific principles by classifying the structures with \nsimilar characteristics and associating them with other concepts (Çepni et al., 1997). In today's \nunderstanding, teaching of the Science course is based on making the information meaningful \nand experi ential for the students rather than the evaluation of the student's level of knowledge \n(MNE, 2018). The most important aim of the Science course is to train students to be science -\nliterate. In this sense, it has been necessitated to use new methods and mak e explanations \naccording to the multiple intelligence level while teaching science. If students are raised as \nscience -literate individuals, they can find rational, logical and concrete solutions to the problems \nthat they face in daily life by scientific me ans. Hence, students know how to access information \nand gain the ability to produce new information. In the process of acquiring these skills, it is \nimportant to endear the Science course to the students (Dönmez, 2017). \nScientific concepts are typically ab stract constructs and they are difficult for students to \nunderstand` may be more appropriate than this sentece ( Çepni & Keleş, 2006). Among the \nreasons for having difficulty, it can be stated that students cannot make information meaningful \nsince they cann ot establish a connection with another situation in their minds (Çepni & Keleş, \n2006). Science teachers should be conscious about this issue and benefit from different methods \nin their lessons. The main objective of the Science course is to provide basic k nowledge and \nskills about environment, earth, physics, chemistry, biology, astronomy, science and \nengineering applications during the primary and secondary education. It is possible to increase \nmeaningful learning when students are enabled to establish the relationships between the \nconcepts in their minds instead of perceiving the concepts as they are so that these skills can be \ngained by the students. When students visualize concepts in their minds, they can establish \nrelationships between the concepts mor e easily. The aim of the Science course is to concretize it \n\"\n\nThere is a typo \"m etaphoric\", \"comparis on\", etc. We should normalize but preserve maybe. Not necessary.\n\nNeed decide facts. Since max 32, can include many. But maybe evaluation expects facts not too many. I'll aim ~30.\n\nPotential facts with citations:\n1. The document identifies itself as a Research Article. W12.\n2. The document's received date is 22.11.2022. W12.\n3. The document's accepted date is 27.12.2022. W12.\n4. The document's published date is 31.12.2022. W12.\n5. The citation given for the article is: Yılmaz, Z. A., & Yaşar, M. D. (2022). Examination of prospective teachers' creative comparisons for the concept of \"science education\". International Journal of Scholars in Education, 5(2), 101-116. W12.\n6. The article title is \"Examination of Prospective Teachers' Creative Comparisons for the Concept of 'Science Education'\". W12.\n7. The article authors are Zeynel Abidin YILMAZ and Mehmet Diyaddin YAŞAR. W12.\n8. Zeynel Abidin Yılmaz is an Assoc. Prof. Dr. at Kilis 7 Aralık University. W12.\n9. Zeynel Abidin Yılmaz's ORCID is 0000-0003-0150-8810. W12.\n10. Zeynel Abidin Yılmaz's email is z07yilmaz@gmail.com. W12.\n11. The document lists an Assoc. Prof. Dr. at Harran University with ORCID 0000-0001-7512-580X. W12.\n12. The document lists an e-posta address mdiyaddinyasar@harran.edu.tr. W12. (Could combine with 11)\n13. The abstract states that determination of prospective teachers' metaphoric perceptions of science education will contribute to the more effective and efficient organization of science education and the organization and structure of its teaching. W12.\n14. The research aimed at determining prospective teachers' metaphoric perceptions toward science education in terms of various variables. W12.\n15. The research was conducted on 301 prospective teachers. W12.\n16. The research adopted a phenomenological study, which is one of the qualitative research designs. W12.\n17. A creative comparison form was used as a data collection tool. W12.\n18. The content analysis method, which is one of the qualitative data analysis methods, was used to analyze the collected data. W12.\n19. As a result, the creative comparisons of prospective teachers for science education were gathere